In article <D40374W.92Jun8215607@kaira.hut.fi>, d40374w@kaira.hut.fi (Tero Valkonen) writes:
>  
>  I came up with some new ideas to NetHack a while back, and I 
> thought I would share them with you (and of course the dev team; 
> I suppose they are reading this newsgroup). I want to hear your 
> comments/criticism about what I have to offer here. In my opinion, 
> the ideas presented below are very much in the spirit of NetHack. 
> That cannot be said about all the new ideas presented on this news- 
> group - I am referring mainly to the phone booth idea here. 
>  
> 1) Mushrooms. A real dungeon must have some plant growth within, 
> and mushrooms fit in the best. Mushrooms could grow everywhere, 
> although they would be most common near water (especially the 
> pools on the lower levels).  
> [...]

We already have molds and fungi... Your mushrooms are basically molds
that are not monsters but just items (i.e. they do not fight back when
attacked). I'd rather say - add new molds, with no attack.
The whole growing business - it is too confusing, and I do not believe 
anybody ever will use it.. Lose a good corpse and 200 turns to get
100 (or even 300) nutrion?

> 2). Slimes. It is only natural to suppose that some kind of slimes 
> also exist in the dungeons. I am suggesting that they only appear 
> in the corridors. Size could vary from 1 to 3 squares.
>[...]

Again nothing new, just another monster of pudding/jelly type...
And be careful with it - if the only way to get rid of it is a fire
(special) attack and it may lurk in a corridor - then in some situations
(especially at low levels, when I have no special attacks and no pickaxe)
you'll separate me from my pet...

> 3) Special monsters. The barlog, the nazgul and the named demons/ 
> devils are way too easy to kill! Suppose that one of them makes 
> 3d6 points of damage. Turn this into 6d3 and make everything else 
> about him harder, too.  

Then, don't allow them to appear randomly. If they are that hard, it
must be possible to avoid them (named devils already appear only in
special rooms, I refer to nazguls).

> 5) Soldiers, beehives, zoos and throne rooms. 
> Usually (if not always) when I enter a room mentioned above,
> all of the creatures inside are sleeping. There is nothing
> particularly wrong with this, but it's ridiculous that none
> of the creatures - with the exception of the one I'm fighting
> at the moment - wake up from the noise! Especially soldiers
> should always have one on guard, and he should blow alert
> when necessary. 

If you have stealth - the better for you. But I agree about soldiers - 
there should always be one guard with a bugle in the barrack. Now, if you
can dispose him before he has a chance to use it...

